"A data protection map identifies where information is plaintext or encrypted and who controls the relevant encryption keys.\n\nUse when: Review encryption termination and key custody across integrations. Explain envelope encryption and decryption authority.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need only network reachability or role membership.\n\nVisual grammar: Data locations distinguish plaintext, ciphertext, and protected transport. Key nodes identify key classes and custodians without revealing material. Arrows label encrypt, decrypt, wrap, unwrap, and transport behavior separately.\n\nBuild: Choose a data class and trace its complete relevant path. Mark storage encryption and transport termination independently. Identify key ownership and the principals allowed to decrypt. Place key-management flows in a separate lane with padded trust boundaries.\n\nCheck: Encrypted transport is not mistaken for encryption at every endpoint. Key custody and data custody are independently clear. Unknown controls and proposed encryption remain explicitly labeled. Key-flow arrows do not overlap data labels or boundary titles.\n\nPitfalls: Using a lock icon as proof of end-to-end confidentiality. Showing a key service without identifying who may invoke decryption.\n\nExample: A service receives protected transport, encrypts an object with a data key, and stores its wrapped key beside ciphertext.\n\nAlternatives: Secret distribution diagram, Threat-model data-flow diagram."
